1. Where the denominator comes from
   Twelve ballots, two seats, so the Hare quota is 12/2 = 6 ballots.
   Allison wins seat 1 with 8 supporters at the top score — 8 units of
   weight to spend a 6-unit quota, so each keeps 1 - 6/8:

     seat 1 -> candidate 0   widest weight 1/4   (3 bits)

   winners: Allison, Doug

   The 4 in that 1/4 was not chosen from any fixed set. It is the total
   weight of whoever happened to tie at the top score — so seat 3's
   denominators would be built from seat 2's weights, and so on down.
   There is no set of denominators to take an lcm over.

2. Eight electorates of exactly the same size
   900 ballots, 18 candidates, 12 seats. Only the votes differ.

     widest denominator, in bits: [31, 31, 32, 33, 39, 33, 35, 27]
     min 27, max 39, spread 12 bits
   Same ballot count, same seats, same candidates. The arithmetic the
   count needs is decided by how people voted, and by nothing you know
   before they vote.

3. The same election, counted two ways
   2000 ballots, 30 candidates, 20 seats. Both additions are exact;
   they differ only in how big a number they build on the way.

     a/b + c/d = (ad+cb)/bd  -> OVERFLOWED at seat 16 (widest so far 44 bits)
     both over lcm(b, d)     -> all 20 seats, widest denominator 68 bits

   Read those two lines together. One counted the whole election; the
   other gave up four seats short. And the widest number the finishing
   count ever had to REPRESENT was 68 bits — barely half of i128's 127.
   The failing count did not run out of room for its answers. It ran
   out building `(ad+cb)/bd`, a product of two denominators that it was
   about to reduce away again.

4. Far enough out, the answer itself stops fitting
   2500 ballots, 35 candidates, 28 seats:

     cross-multiply  -> OVERFLOWED at seat 16 (widest so far 33 bits)
     lcm             -> OVERFLOWED at seat 27 (widest so far 73 bits)

   The better addition bought eleven seats, not safety. No fixed width
   covers an unbounded quantity, and the width this count needs is not
   knowable until it has read the ballots — which is why it returns an
   Outcome rather than a number.
